METHOD
For each half-level K through 30, affine wall n through 4, reduced
next factor q, and row r, the analyzer constructs the exact linear
global-payment functional Lambda on ordinary SU(2) profiles.

For intervals I=[A,B] and J=[C,D], let 1_I and 1_J denote their
ordinary-character coefficient vectors.  The checked bilinear value
is

  B(I,J)=Lambda(1_I 1_J).

For every inner interval J contained in indices 0 through 120, the
analyzer minimizes B(I,J) over every containing outer interval I.
For fixed J, extending I to the left and right contributes independent
row sums of the exact pair kernel, so the minimum is the core square
J x J plus the independently minimal left and right extensions.
Consequently one optimized check covers every I containing J, rather
than sampling one outer interval.

All 142,896,160 optimized minima are nonnegative.  Since the payment
bilinear form is symmetric, this covers both possible orders of every
nested interval pair in the bounded box.  The result is bounded
evidence for the uniform nested-interval inequality, not its proof.

The same run checks three exact period reductions.  It verifies every
separated full-period outer block, every full-period inner-width
increment after minimizing the left extension, and every interaction
between one point and one interval of length L.  Their respective
counts are 2,863,623,296, 1,431,811,648, and 174,162,560, all without
a failure.  Lemma AIMPP and Corollary AIMPB prove the point-period
statement unboundedly; the larger block counts independently audit
its bilinear consequences.

The optimizer also records separately whether the inner square, its
best contiguous left extension, or its best contiguous right extension
is negative.  Every one of those five diagnostic counts is zero:
the full nested inequality does not rely on cancellation between a
negative core and positive outer extensions in the bounded box.

The implementation forms the pair kernel exactly from

  sum_(t=|a-b|)^(a+b) eta_(n;q,r)(t),

and uses signed 64-bit integer arithmetic.  In the displayed bounds,
kernel entries and rectangle sums are far below the signed limit.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror \
  analyze_su2_aim_nested_intervals.cpp \
  -o analyze_su2_aim_nested_intervals

